Well, it tries to find _a_ match for what it's given. In this case, it
found one that wasn't intended; the argument would be that the OP should
express his intentions more accurately.

(On the other hand, with regard to this particular instance, I
wouldn't object to "mindflayer" and "master mindflayer" being added to
the alt_spl struct in name_to_mon().)

> Maybe it would be better 
> to exclude titles from the search list for genosearch,
> or to insist of exact names and don't do any parsing 
> at all.  (What's the reason for this, anyway?  Is the 
> DevTeam getting soft?)

There has to be at least minimal parsing, since it has to cope with
singulars and plurals; it'd be unfair to allow 'newt' and disallow
'newts', or 'mumak' and disallow 'mumakil'. Similarly, you want to
allow both 'gray dragon' and 'grey dragon'. Once you've got that far,
you might as well be using the general monster-name parsing function
that doubles for polymorph control as well, and also for wishing for
things that involve monsters ("tin of orc captain meat", "figurine of
a sewer rat").

Having said that, I don't think rank titles are one of the things that
_have_ to be grokked by name_to_mon(), but I may be missing some place
where it's used internally.

